### 4. Managing Dependencies Between Controls (Show/Hide)

**Problem:** You have a control that should only be visible if another setting has a specific value. (e.g., Show "Custom Header Height" only if "Header Layout" is set to "Custom").

**Solution:** Use the **`active_callback`** parameter in your control.

```php
// First, create the main setting (the one that controls the visibility)
$wp_customize->add_setting( 'header_layout', array(
    'default'   => 'default',
    'transport' => 'refresh',
) );

$wp_customize->add_control( 'header_layout', array(
    'label'   => __( 'Header Layout', 'my-theme' ),
    'section' => 'header_section',
    'type'    => 'select',
    'choices' => array(
        'default' => 'Default',
        'custom'  => 'Custom Layout',
    ),
) );

// Now, create the dependent setting
$wp_customize->add_setting( 'custom_header_height', array(
    'default' => 100,
    'transport' => 'postMessage',
) );

$wp_customize->add_control( 'custom_header_height', array(
    'label'           => __( 'Custom Header Height (px)', 'my-theme' ),
    'section'         => 'header_section',
    'type'            => 'number',
    'active_callback' => 'is_custom_header_layout' // This function determines visibility
) );

// Define the active_callback function
function is_custom_header_layout( $control ) {
    // Return true if the 'header_layout' setting is set to 'custom'
    return ( $control->manager->get_setting( 'header_layout' )->value() === 'custom' );
}
```

Now, the "Custom Header Height" control will magically appear only when "Header Layout" is set to "Custom Layout".

