### 🎨 Immediate Fix: Resize the Logo with Custom CSS
Adding the following CSS code will make your logo larger. You can add it by going to **Appearance > Customize > Additional CSS** in your WordPress dashboard.

```css
/* Make the main logo larger in the header */
body .at-site-header .site-branding .custom-logo {
    max-width: 250px !important; /* Adjust this value as needed */
    height: auto !important;
}

/* Optional: Ensure the logo looks good on mobile devices */
@media (max-width: 480px) {
    body .at-site-header .site-branding .custom-logo {
        max-width: 180px !important;
    }
}
```

- **How it works:** This code targets the logo image with high specificity and increases its maximum width. The `!important` rule ensures it overrides any existing styles[citation:7].
- **Customizing the size:** Simply change the `250px` value to whatever looks best for your layout. For reference, a width between **200px and 350px** is common for website headers[citation:1][citation:4].

### ⚙️ Long-Term Solution: Implement Customizer Number Inputs
The CSS fix above is a great temporary measure, but for full flexibility, the logo size and padding controls in the theme's Customizer should be functional number inputs. Here’s a summary of how to implement this:

| Control Feature | Implementation Suggestion |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Control Type** | Use HTML `input` with `type="number"` for precise values. |
| **Live Preview** | Use **`postMessage`** transport for instant preview without a full page refresh[citation:7]. |
| **Suggested Defaults** | **Max Width:** ~200px (allowing up to 500px+).<br>**Max Height:** ~60px (flexible range).<br>**Padding:** 0px (with reasonable upper limit). |

This approach aligns with how other major themes (like Astra and Ultra) handle logo resizing, giving users direct input boxes to type their desired dimensions[citation:7].

### 📝 Tips for Best Results
