### 🏗️ Leverage Proven Header Structures with Flexbox

Modern website headers are commonly built with CSS Flexbox because it efficiently handles alignment, spacing, and responsiveness[citation:2]. Here are a few working variations you can use as a starting point.

#### **Variation 1: Basic Logo & Navigation**
This is a standard, clean structure using `justify-content: space-between` to push the logo and nav apart[citation:2].

```html
<header class="at-site-header">
  <div class="site-header__wrapper">
    <a href="#" class="site-branding">
      <img src="logo.svg" alt="Brand" class="custom-logo"/>
    </a>
    <nav class="primary-navigation">
      <!-- Navigation Menu -->
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>
```

```css
.site-header__wrapper {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
  flex-wrap: wrap; /* Prevents horizontal scrolling on small screens */
}
```

#### **Variation 2: Logo, Navigation, and a Button**
When you add a third element like a button, using `margin-left: auto` on the navigation is a reliable method for spacing[citation:2].

```html
<header class="at-site-header">
  <div class="site-header__wrapper">
    <a href="#" class="site-branding"><img src="logo.svg" alt="Brand" class="custom-logo"/></a>
    <nav class="primary-navigation"></nav>
    <a href="/contact" class="button cta-button">Contact Us</a>
  </div>
</header>
```

```css
.site-header__wrapper {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 1rem; /* Easily adds spacing between all child items */
}
.primary-navigation {
  margin-left: auto; /* Pushes the nav and button to the right */
}
```

#### **Variation 3: Centered Logo with Side Elements**
This layout is trickier but achievable by giving each section a `flex: 1` and aligning them appropriately[citation:2].

```html
<header class="at-site-header">
  <div class="site-header__wrapper">
    <nav class="secondary-navigation"></nav>
    <a href="#" class="site-branding"><img src="logo.svg" alt="Brand" class="custom-logo"/></a>
    <div class="header-actions">
      <a href="/contact" class="button">Contact Us</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>
```

```css
.site-header__wrapper {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}
.site-header__wrapper > * {
  flex: 1; /* Makes each section take up equal width */
}
.secondary-navigation {
  text-align: start;
}
.site-branding {
  text-align: center;
}
.header-actions {
  text-align: end;
}
```

### 💡 Key Technical Takeaways for Your Theme

Based on the analysis of these patterns, here are the core concepts to implement in your Abundant Thought theme:

*   **Use an Inner Wrapper**: Always contain your header elements within a `div` wrapper. This allows for constraining the maximum width and managing padding without affecting the full-width background, which is crucial for large screens[citation:2].
*   **Employ `flex-wrap`**: Always include `flex-wrap: wrap` in your Flexbox rules. This is a defensive CSS tactic that prevents horizontal scrolling if the content becomes too wide for the viewport[citation:2].
*   **Utilize the `gap` Property**: For modern browsers, the `gap` property is the simplest way to add consistent spacing between all items in your header without using margins on individual elements[citation:2].
*   **Study a Complete Reference**: For a deep dive into every Flexbox property, the CSS-Tricks "A Complete Guide to Flexbox" is an industry-standard resource[citation:7].

